---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import PageNext from '../../components/PageNext.astro';
import CtaBlock from '../../components/CtaBlock.astro';
import CapabilityMatrix from '../../components/CapabilityMatrix.astro';
import { DOCTOR_GATES } from '../../data/stats';
---

<Layout title="Máddu + Codex CLI" pathname="/compare/codex-cli">
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="eyebrow-chain">
        <span class="eyebrow">Compare</span>
        <span class="eyebrow-sep">·</span>
        <span class="eyebrow-dim">CLI coding agents</span>
        <span class="eyebrow-sep">·</span>
        <span class="eyebrow-dim">layer on top</span>
      </div>
      <h1>Máddu + Codex CLI. <span class="dim-h1">The governance mode OpenAI declined to build.</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        In April 2026 someone asked OpenAI for a persistent governed repo mode for long-running local workflows. It was closed as not planned. Codex still shipped the lifecycle hooks — the exact attachment points Máddu uses to build that mode anyway.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">§ 01 · What Codex CLI is</span>
        <h2>A strong agent with sandboxing built in.</h2>
        <p>
          Codex CLI is OpenAI's terminal coding agent — roughly 95k GitHub stars as of July 2026 — and a genuinely capable one, with OS-level sandboxing and approval modes shipped in the core. It also ships lifecycle hooks: PreToolUse, PermissionRequest, PostToolUse, PreCompact and PostCompact, SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit, and Stop. What it does not ship is a persistent, governed repo mode. Issue #18273 asked for exactly that in April 2026, and OpenAI closed it as not planned.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="vs-honest">
        <h4>Where Codex CLI is stronger — plainly</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>It is a strong, fast-moving agent with a large community and OpenAI's release cadence behind it.</li>
          <li>Native OS sandboxing and approval modes ship in the core — real process boundaries Máddu deliberately does not attempt.</li>
          <li>Its hooks are first-party and free; you don't need Máddu to make them fire.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">§ 02 · Where Máddu differs</span>
        <h2>OpenAI declined the mode. Máddu is the mode.</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="panel">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <span class="panel-title">The persistent governed mode</span>
            <span class="panel-aside">what #18273 asked for</span>
          </div>
          <p>Long-running local workflows that carry state across sessions: exclusive lane claims, an approvals ledger, cost budgets, and a durable record. That is the request OpenAI declined — and it is, almost line for line, what Máddu is. The hooks Codex ships are how Máddu attaches.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <span class="panel-title">Deterministic gates</span>
            <span class="panel-aside">{DOCTOR_GATES} + ci</span>
          </div>
          <p>Sandboxing bounds what the agent <em>may</em> do at the OS boundary. Máddu checks what it <em>did</em>: {DOCTOR_GATES} deterministic gates, a headless <code>maddu ci</code> rail that goes red only on pinned gates, and a completion-claim check that demands observed proof for every "done".</p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <span class="panel-title">A repo-owned spine</span>
            <span class="panel-aside">tamper-evident</span>
          </div>
          <p>Máddu's history is a hash-chained, append-only spine in <code>.maddu/</code> — auditable with <code>cat</code>, verified with <code>maddu spine verify</code>, and portable across runtimes. Swap Codex for another agent tomorrow and the record, the policy, and the earned trust all carry over.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <Callout tone="accent" title="Cooperative, never a proxy" tag="the framing">
        <p>
          Máddu doesn't wrap Codex, sit in its request path, or duplicate its sandbox — it records, gates, and verifies from beside the agent, hanging off the same lifecycle hooks Codex already fires. The persistent governed repo mode is real; OpenAI just decided it wasn't theirs to build.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <CapabilityMatrix
        players={['codex-cli']}
        eyebrow="§ 03 · The matrix"
        title="Axis by axis."
        subtitle="The fifteen research axes, focused on this matchup. Conservative marks — shipped, documented surfaces only."
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">§ 04 · Choose honestly</span>
        <h2>Keep the agent. Add the mode.</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="panel">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <span class="panel-title">Codex CLI alone is enough when</span>
          </div>
          <ul class="vs-list">
            <li>You want a strong agent with OS sandboxing and nothing more</li>
            <li>Work is short-lived — no long-running state to carry across sessions</li>
            <li>No one needs a durable, portable record of what was allowed</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <span class="panel-title">Add Máddu when</span>
          </div>
          <ul class="vs-list">
            <li>You want the persistent governed repo mode #18273 asked for</li>
            <li>You need lanes, an approvals ledger, and deterministic gates around the work</li>
            <li>The spine must survive the session and travel with the repo</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="feat-note">
        Related: <a href="/features/worktrees">worktree lanes</a> · <a href="/features/autonomy">earned autonomy</a> · <a href="/features/ci">governance in CI</a> · <a href="/hard-rules">the hard rules</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <PageNext
    eyebrow="Next comparison"
    title="Máddu vs Paperclip."
    hint="The one head-on competitor — and the record is the difference."
    href="/compare/paperclip"
    tone="danger"
  />
  <CtaBlock />
</Layout>

<style>
  .vs-honest {
    padding: var(--m-space-4) var(--m-space-5);
    background: var(--m-bg-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--m-line);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--m-warn);
    border-radius: var(--m-radius);
    max-width: 720px;
  }
  .vs-honest h4 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--m-space-2);
    font-family: var(--m-font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--m-warn);
  }
  .vs-honest ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 18px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 6px; }
  .vs-honest li { font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--m-fg-1); }
  .vs-list { margin: 0; padding-left: 18px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; }
  .vs-list li { font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--m-fg-1); line-height: 1.5; }
  .feat-note {
    margin-top: var(--m-space-5);
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--m-fg-2);
  }
  .feat-note code { font-size: 12px; }
</style>
